{
  "title": "CourseLit \u2014 EdTech / E-Learning (1,161\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: CourseLit\nDomain: EdTech / E-Learning | Scale: medium | Stars: 1,161\nProblem: An open-source learning management system that enables creators to sell online courses, digital downloads, and publish blogs on their own branded websites. Provides a self-hosted alternative to proprietary platforms like Teachable, Thinkific, and Podia.\nTarget users: Online course creators, Digital product sellers, Educational content publishers, Small to medium educational businesses, Self-hosted platform operators\nCore features: Course authoring and management; Student management and enrollment; Payment processing via Stripe; Website builder with custom sales pages; Blog publishing and CMS; Analytics dashboard; Custom widget extensibility\nIntegrations: Stripe (payment processing), MediaLit (media asset management), Vercel (deployment), MongoDB (database)\nTech stack: React, TypeScript, CSS + Node.js, TypeScript + MongoDB + Vercel, Docker, pnpm workspaces\nKey libraries: Next.js (implied from Vercel deployment), Jest (testing), ESLint, Prettier (linting/formatting), Astro (documentation/static content), MDX (content authoring)\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Monorepo structure with pnpm workspaces enables code sharing across packages and apps (web, docs) while maintaining separation of concerns\n- Batteries-included philosophy: pre-packages essential features (payments, media, analytics) rather than relying on plugins, reducing complexity for end-users\n- Custom widget system allows extensibility without forking the core codebase, supporting community-driven feature expansion\n- Self-hosting support with documented migration scripts and environment configuration demonstrates commitment to platform independence and data sovereignty",
